## Proof

**Answer: Yes, the identity holds with probability one.**

We establish this via Lévy's identity and the law of the iterated logarithm.

**Step 1 (Lévy's identity).** Let $M_t = \sup_{0 \le s \le t} B_s$. A classical theorem of Lévy states that the two-dimensional processes have the same law:
$$\bigl(|B_t|,\, L_t\bigr)_{t \ge 0} \;\equiv\; \bigl(M_t - B_t,\, M_t\bigr)_{t \ge 0}.$$
In particular, the one-dimensional processes $(L_t)_{t\ge 0}$ and $(M_t)_{t\ge 0}$ have the same law. Consequently the random variable $\limsup_{t\to 0} L_t/\sqrt{t}$ has the same distribution as $\limsup_{t\to 0} M_t/\sqrt{t}$.

**Step 2 (LIL at zero).** By Kolmogorov's law of the iterated logarithm for Brownian motion,
$$\limsup_{t \to 0} \frac{B_t}{\sqrt{2t \log\log(1/t)}} = 1, \qquad \text{a.s.}$$

**Step 3 (Transfer to $M_t$).** Since $M_t = \sup_{0 \le s \le t} B_s \ge B_t$ for every $t$, we have
$$\limsup_{t \to 0} \frac{M_t}{\sqrt{2t \log\log(1/t)}} \;\ge\; \limsup_{t \to 0} \frac{B_t}{\sqrt{2t \log\log(1/t)}} \;=\; 1, \qquad \text{a.s.}$$
Writing
$$\frac{M_t}{\sqrt{t}} \;=\; \frac{M_t}{\sqrt{2t\log\log(1/t)}} \cdot \sqrt{2\log\log(1/t)},$$
and noting that $\sqrt{2\log\log(1/t)} \to \infty$ as $t \to 0$, the lower bound of $1$ on the first factor forces
$$\limsup_{t \to 0} \frac{M_t}{\sqrt{t}} \;=\; \infty, \qquad \text{a.s.}$$

**Step 4 (Conclusion).** Since $(L_t)_{t\ge 0} \equiv (M_t)_{t\ge 0}$ in law as processes, the functional $\limsup_{t\to 0} L_t/\sqrt{t}$ has the same distribution as $\limsup_{t\to 0} M_t/\sqrt{t}$. The latter is almost surely the constant $\infty$, hence
$$\limsup_{t \to 0} \frac{L_t}{\sqrt{t}} \;=\; \infty, \qquad \text{a.s.}$$

